  "articleSummary": "The article argues that hiring AI engineers is deceptive because a smooth demo can hide critical failures, such as handling double refunds or cost control. The core thesis is that hiring managers must evaluate a candidate’s judgment and ability to own real decisions by defining the specific role first, then using a paid working session with real work to observe how they approach evaluation, failure recovery, and safety boundaries. Written in a practical, advisory tone for engineering managers and founders, it emphasizes “opening the hood” on projects—asking about what broke, how measurement works, and designing for idempotency and escalation—rather than relying on résumés or polished demos. The framing uses metaphors like “open the hood” to contrast surface-level showmanship with substantive engineering judgment.",
